        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-104309",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{3-2x}{2{x}^{2}-2}$, find ${f}^{'}(x).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=3-2x$ and $v(x)=2{x}^{2}-2,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =-2, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)(2{x}^{2}-2)-(3-2x)(4x)}{{(2{x}^{2}-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{x}^{2}+4-12x+8{x}^{2}}{{(2{x}^{2}-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{4{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+1}{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
      {
