        ":question/id": "a97b1e20-63fd-5ba5-b6fc-1f82595d60c6",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-340290",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$, find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=2$ and $v(x)={x}^{2},$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =0, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
      {
