        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-340287",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$, find ${f}^{\u2032}(x).$\n\n${f}^{\u2032}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=1$ and $v(x)=x+1,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{\u2032}(x) & =0, \\\\ {v}^{\u2032}(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{\u2032}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (x+1)-1\\cdot 1}{{(x+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{(x+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{(x+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
