        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-340303",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ find ${f}^{\u2032}(x).$\n\n${f}^{\u2032}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{\u2032}(x) & =1, \\\\ {v}^{\u2032}(x) & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{\u2032}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-x\\cdot (2x)}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      }
